mplementation in the yeast <italic>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</italic>. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>271</volume>:<fpage>9801</fpage>&#x02013;9808.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">8621662</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><citation citation-type="journal">Hogue, D.L., L. Kerby, and V. Ling. <year>1999</year>. A mammalian lysosomal membrane protein confers multidrug resistance upon expression in Saccharomyces cerevisiae. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>274</volume>:<fpage>12877</fpage>&#x02013;12882.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10212276</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><citation citation-type="journal">Hogue, D.L., C. Nash, V. Ling, and T.C. Hobman. <year>2002</year>. Lysosome-associated protein transmembrane 4 alpha (LAPTM4 alpha) requires two tandemly arranged tyrosine-based signals for sorting to lysosomes. <source>Biochem. J.</source> <volume>365</volume>:<fpage>721</fpage>&#x02013;730.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11980562</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><citation citation-type="journal">Huizing, M., R. Sarangarajan, E. Strovel, Y. Zhao, W.A. Gahl, and R.E. Boissy. <year>2001</year>. AP-3 mediates tyrosinase but not TRP-1 trafficking in human melanocytes. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>12</volume>:<fpage>2075</fpage>&#x02013;2085.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11452004</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><citation citation-type="journal">Janvier, K., and J.S. Bonifacino. <year>2005</year>. Role of the endocytic machinery in the sorting of lysosome-associated membrane proteins. <source>Mol. Biol. Cell.</source> <volume>16</volume>:<fpage>4231</fpage>&#x02013;4242.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15987739</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib18"><citation citation-type="journal">Janvier, K., Y. Kato, M. Boehm, J.R. Rose, J.A. Martina, B.Y. Kim, S. Venkatesan, and J.S. Bonifacino. <year>2003</year>. Recognition of dileucine-based sorting signals from HIV-1 Nef and LIMP-II by the AP-1 &#x003b3;-&#x003c3;1 and AP-3 &#x003b4;-&#x003c3;3 hemicomplexes. <source>J. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>163</volume>:<fpage>1281</fpage>&#x02013;1290.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">14691137</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib19"><citation citation-type="journal">Kametaka, S., R. Mattera, and J.S. Bonifacino. <year>2005</year>. Epidermal growth factor-dependent phosphorylation of the GGA3 adaptor protein regulates its recruitment to membranes. <source>Mol. Cell. Biol.</source> <volume>25</volume>:<fpage>7988</fpage>&#x02013;8000.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16135791</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib20"><citation citation-type="journal">Katz, M., K. Shtiegman, P. Tal-Or, L. Yakir, Y. Mosesson, D. Harari, Y. Machluf, H. Asao, T. Jovin, K. Sugamura, and Y. Yarden. <year>2002</year>. Ligand-independent degradation of epidermal growth factor receptor involves receptor ubiquitylation and Hgs, an adaptor whose ubiquitin-interacting motif targets ubiquitylation by Nedd4. <source>Traffic.</source> <volume>3</volume>:<fpage>740</fpage>&#x02013;751.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12230472</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib21"><citation citation-type="journal">Katzmann, D.J., M. Babst, and S.D. Emr. <year>2001</year>. Ubiquitin-dependent sorting into the multivesicular body pathway requires the function of a conserved endosomal protein sorting complex, ESCRT-I. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>106</volume>:<fpage>145</fpage>&#x02013;155.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11511343</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib22"><citation citation-type="journal">Katzmann, D.J., G. Odorizzi, and S.D. Emr. <year>2002</year>. Receptor downregulation and multivesicular-body sorting. <source>Nat. Rev. Mol. Cell Biol.</source> <volume>3</volume>:<fpage>893</fpage>&#x02013;905.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12461556</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib23"><citation citation-type="journal">Kawasaki, M., T. Shiba, Y. Shiba, Y. Yamaguchi, N. Matsugaki, N. Igarashi, M. Suzuki, R. Kato, K. Kato, K. Nakayama, and S. Wakatsuki. <year>2005</year>. Molecular mechanism of ubiquitin recognition by GGA3 GAT domain. <source>Genes Cells.</source> <volume>10</volume>:<fpage>639</fpage>&#x02013;654.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15966896</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib24"><citation citation-type="journal">Levkowitz, G., H. Waterman, E. Zamir, Z. Kam, S. Oved, W.Y. Langdon, L. Beguinot, B. Geiger, and Y. Yarden. <year>1998</year>. c-Cbl/Sli-1 regulates endocytic sorting and ubiquitination of the epidermal growth factor receptor. <source>Genes Dev.</source> <volume>12</volume>:<fpage>3663</fpage>&#x02013;3674.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">9851973</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib25"><citation citation-type="journal">Miller, S.L., E. Malotky, and J.P. O'Bryan. <year>2004</year>. Analysis of the role of ubiquitin-interacting motifs in ubiquitin binding and ubiquitylation. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>279</volume>:<fpage>33528</fpage>&#x02013;33537.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15155768</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib26"><citation citation-type="journal">Misra, S., R. Puertollano, Y. Kato, J.S. Bonifacino, and J.H. Hurley. <year>2002</year>. Structural basis for acidic-cluster-dileucine sorting-signal recognition by VHS domains. <source>Nature.</source> <volume>415</volume>:<fpage>933</fpage>&#x02013;937.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11859375</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal">Mosesson, Y., K. Shtiegman, M. Katz, Y. Zwang, G. Vereb, J. Szollosi, and Y. Yarden. <year>2003</year>. Endocytosis of receptor tyrosine kinases is driven by monoubiquitylation, not polyubiquitylation. <source>J. Biol. Chem.</source> <volume>278</volume>:<fpage>21323</fpage>&#x02013;21326.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12719435</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal">Odorizzi, G., M. Babst, and S.D. Emr. <year>1998</year>. Fab1p PtdIns(3)P 5-kinase function essential for protein sorting in the multivesicular body. <source>Cell.</source> <volume>95</volume>:<fpage>847</fpage>&#x0